      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 SEICAP. Published by Elsevier España, S.L.U. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Food allergy (FA) prevalence is well documented in developed countries and appears to be increasing, but remains unknown in most Latin American countries. We aimed to evaluate on a population basis the parent-reported prevalence of FA and its clinical characteristics in Mexican schoolchildren."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "A validated Spanish version of a structured written questionnaire was administered to parents of schoolchildren aged 5-13 years old from Culiacan, Mexico."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "A total of 1049 parents responded to the survey (response rate, 84%). The estimated prevalence rates (95% CI) were: adverse food reactions 10.0% (8.3-11.9), \"perceived FA, ever\" 5.5% (4.3-7.0), \"physician-diagnosed FA, ever\" 4.9% (3.7-6.3), \"immediate-type FA, ever\" 4.4% (3.3-5.8), \"immediate-type FA, current\" 3.5% (2.6-4.8), and anaphylaxis 1.2% (0.72-2.1). Immediate hypersensitivity reactions were mainly triggered by the consumption of shrimp (1.3%), other shellfish (0.7%), strawberry (0.6%), chocolate (0.5%), and egg (0.4%). Schoolchildren with \"immediate-type FA, current\" had more atopic dermatitis and allergic rhinitis (p<0.05), but not asthma or drug allergy (p>0.05) than children without FA. All cases of anaphylaxis sought medical attention, but only one child had physician-diagnosed anaphylaxis and was advised to acquire an epinephrine autoinjector."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "The prevalence of \"immediate-type FA, current\" to any food is 3.5% in Mexican schoolchildren. The poor recognition of anaphylaxis and the low frequency of prescription of epinephrine autoinjectors suggest that acute food-induced allergic reactions are not optimally managed in Mexico."
          }
        ]
      },
